  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 1236 AM MDT Sat Aug 1 2026\n\nThere has been changes in the guidance next week and the\npotential for heavier precipitation. The GFS, while still\ndeveloping periods of rainfall over southern Colorado, has\ndried out, more in line with the ECMWF and various ensemble\nguidance.\n\nSunday and Monday...the upper high over the Desert Southwest is\nforecast to stretch across Colorado and into the Northern\nPlains, as an upper trough digs into the Pacific Northwest. This\nwill allow for drier air to settle across Colorado both days,\nwith generally isolated to scattered thunderstorms across the\nhigher terrain under the upper ridge. Lightning and gusty\noutflow winds will be the main concerns with any cells that\ndevelop. Overall, coverage looks pretty minimal on Sunday, with\nslightly better coverage across the Mountains on Monday. The\nmain concern for Sunday and Monday will be the heat. Heat\nAdvisories are already out for both days for El Paso, Pueblo and\neastern Fremont Counties. Heat advisories may need to be added\nfor the lower Arkansas Valley on Monday, when highs will be\npushing 105F. For Sunday, widespread mid 90s to lower 100s are\nexpected. Further warming is forecast for Monday, with upper 90s\nto lower 100s on the Plains. Be prepared for the heat by\nlimiting outdoor activities, staying hydrated, and having\nsomeplace cool to go if needed.\n\nTuesday through Saturday...models have changed a bit in the past\n24 hours. All still produce the longwave trough moving out of\nthe Pacific Northwest and into the Northern Plains, squeezing\nthe upper high over Arizona and New Mexico. Tuesday and\nWednesday still look like days with the highest shower and\nthunderstorm coverage. However, guidance is weaker with the \ncold front Tuesday morning with less upslope flow, and a delay \nin PWAT values returning to Colorado. There won't be a lack of \nembedded waves in the westerly flow across Colorado, with near \ndaily waves Tuesday through Thursday. For now, maintained the \nNBM output given the changes in the models this evening. But, \nmid week will need to be watched, especially for burn scar flash\nflooding, and low end stronger thunderstorm potential. \n\nBy Friday and Saturday, the upper trough continues east into the\nGreat Lakes, and the upper high to our south looks to begin\nbuilding back northward. Monsoonal moisture will be in place,\nand continued shower and thunderstorm activity is forecast\nacross the state.  \n\nTemperatures will cool down for Tuesday through Thursday, with\n80s and lower 90s across the region. As the upper ridge rebuilds\noverhead Friday into Saturday, temperatures will once again warm\nback to near 100 on the Plains.",
